Hand an interface to olr, so that it may serve addresses, answer DNS or
route traffic on it.

Adopting by itself changes nothing on the machine: no address is set, no
daemon is started, and the interface keeps whatever configuration it
already had. What it changes is permission — olr refuses to touch an
interface nobody handed it (design.md §3.4), so this is the step that
makes `olr dhcp add pool` stop refusing.

Usage:
  olr adopt <interface> [flags]

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for adopt

Global Flags:
      --dry-run         show what would change and exit without applying it
  -o, --output string   output format: text|json (default "text")
      --socket string   olrd control socket (default "/run/olr/olrd.sock")
